Q: How to access bash variable in python script via hql file(bash-hql-python) A: <pre><code>/* ============================================================
   SCRIPT TASK 2a: &quot;Resolve FileDate&quot;
   ============================================================
   SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY: resolve which business date this run
   is processing. That is all this task does.

   No file system access. No path building. No external calls.
   This task cannot fail due to environmental conditions —
   it only reads variables and does a date calculation.
   This guarantees FileDate is always valid before anything
   that can actually fail (file check, staging, target load).

   WHY THIS IS SEPARATE FROM FILE VERIFICATION:
   Previously, date resolution and file checking were in one
   task. If the file didn't exist, the task failed before
   FileDate was ever set — leaving the OnError handler with
   no valid FileDate to write to ETLJobLoadStat. Splitting
   them means FileDate is always available to OnError,
   regardless of what fails downstream.

   SSIS VARIABLES:

   ReadOnlyVariables:
     User::IsManualBackfill          (Boolean)
     User::FileDateOverride          (String)   'yyyyMMdd' or blank
     User::IsFileNameDateConstrained (Boolean)  from ETLJobMgmt
     User::IsFileContentDateConstrained (Boolean) from ETLJobMgmt
     User::NextExpectedDateValue     (DateTime) from ETLJobMgmt
     User::LoadID                    (Int64)    for error messages

   ReadWriteVariables:
     User::FileDate                  (DateTime) output of this task
   ============================================================ */

using System;
using Microsoft.SqlServer.Dts.Runtime;

public void Main()
{
    try
    {
        bool     isBackfill    = (bool)Dts.Variables[&quot;User::IsManualBackfill&quot;].Value;
        string   dateOverride  = Dts.Variables[&quot;User::FileDateOverride&quot;].Value.ToString();
        bool     isFileNameDateConstrained    = (bool)Dts.Variables[&quot;User::IsFileNameDateConstrained&quot;].Value;
        bool     isFileContentDateConstrained = (bool)Dts.Variables[&quot;User::IsFileContentDateConstrained&quot;].Value;
        DateTime nextExpected  = (DateTime)Dts.Variables[&quot;User::NextExpectedDateValue&quot;].Value;
        long     loadID        = (long)Dts.Variables[&quot;User::LoadID&quot;].Value;

        DateTime targetDate;

        if (isBackfill &amp;&amp; !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(dateOverride))
        {
            // Manual backfill: human-supplied date overrides everything.
            // FileDateOverride is String — passed via /Set from the bat
            // file which can only pass string values.
            if (!DateTime.TryParseExact(dateOverride, &quot;yyyyMMdd&quot;,
                    System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                    System.Globalization.DateTimeStyles.None, out targetDate))
            {
                Dts.Events.FireError(0, &quot;Resolve FileDate&quot;,
                    string.Format(&quot;[LoadID:{0}] FileDateOverride '{1}' is not a &quot; +
                        &quot;valid yyyyMMdd date. Backfill aborted.&quot;, loadID, dateOverride),
                    &quot;&quot;, 0);
                Dts.TaskResult = (int)ScriptResults.Failure;
                return;
            }
        }
        else if (isFileNameDateConstrained)
        {
            // Normal case: date comes from NextExpectedDateValue.
            // NOT DateTime.Today — this is the critical design decision.
            // .Date strips any time component the DateTime variable may carry.
            targetDate = nextExpected.Date;
        }
        else if (isFileContentDateConstrained)
        {
            // NOT YET IMPLEMENTED.
            // Fails loudly — do not replace with DateTime.Today.
            Dts.Events.FireError(0, &quot;Resolve FileDate&quot;,
                string.Format(&quot;[LoadID:{0}] IsFileContentDateConstrained is not &quot; +
                    &quot;yet implemented for this job. Add file-content date extraction &quot; +
                    &quot;logic before enabling in production.&quot;, loadID),
                &quot;&quot;, 0);
            Dts.TaskResult = (int)ScriptResults.Failure;
            return;
        }
        else
        {
            // Neither flag set: CalendarDayRun-style job.
            // Only legitimate use of DateTime.Today in this template.
            targetDate = DateTime.Today;

            Dts.Events.FireInformation(0, &quot;Resolve FileDate&quot;,
                string.Format(&quot;[LoadID:{0}] Neither date constraint flag is set. &quot; +
                    &quot;Defaulting FileDate to today ({1}). If this job should be &quot; +
                    &quot;date-driven, update ETLJobMgmt flags.&quot;,
                    loadID, DateTime.Today.ToString(&quot;yyyy-MM-dd&quot;)),
                &quot;&quot;, 0, ref boolPassThrough);
        }

        // .Date applied to strip any time component
        Dts.Variables[&quot;User::FileDate&quot;].Value = targetDate.Date;

        Dts.Events.FireInformation(0, &quot;Resolve FileDate&quot;,
            string.Format(&quot;[LoadID:{0}] FileDate resolved: {1}&quot;,
                loadID, targetDate.ToString(&quot;yyyy-MM-dd&quot;)),
            &quot;&quot;, 0, ref boolPassThrough);

        Dts.TaskResult = (int)ScriptResults.Success;
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        long loadID = 0;
        try { loadID = (long)Dts.Variables[&quot;User::LoadID&quot;].Value; } catch { }

        Dts.Events.FireError(0, &quot;Resolve FileDate&quot;,
            string.Format(&quot;[LoadID:{0}] Unhandled exception: {1}&quot;, loadID, ex.Message),
            &quot;&quot;, 0);
        Dts.TaskResult = (int)ScriptResults.Failure;
    }
}

private bool boolPassThrough = false;
</code></pre>

Q: Angular Material 3 Light and Dark modes A: <p>My problem is caused by <a href="https://caniuse.com/?search=light-dark" rel="nofollow noreferrer">browser support of the <code>light-dark()</code> css function</a>.  The phone I'm testing it on only has iOS 16 and that isn't supported until iOS 17.4 which was released on March 5, 2024.  It seems pretty funky for angular material to not at least have some explanation of this on their pages since the feature is so new and still isn't supported by 10% of browser usage.</p>
<p>I still want to support the order style, so I split out all the theme declarations into two different files so I could do it both ways.   My styles.scss now starts like this and I can use the variables in my styles:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@use 'styles_older.scss';
@use 'styles_newer.scss';
body { font: var(--mat-sys-body-medium); }
</code></pre>
<p>In <code>styles_newer.scss</code> I wrap everything in a <code>@supports</code> block so it will only apply if the browser supports <code>light-dark()</code> and don't specify <code>theme-type</code>.   The theme will then use the <code>light-dark()</code> function.  This makes it SUPER EASY to make sure the styles apply using inheritance.   The <code>...</code> signify code for the theme I omitted for clarity.</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@supports (background-color: light-dark(#fff, #000)) {
  :root {
    color-scheme: light dark;
    @include mat.theme((color: (primary: mat.$azure-palette,...
  }

  .light { color-scheme: only light }
  .dark { color-scheme: only dark }
  .green {
    @include mat.theme((color: (primary: mat.$green-palette,...
  }
  .orange {
    @include mat.theme((color: (primary: mat.$orange-palette,...
  }

}
</code></pre>
<p>I wrap the code in <code>styles_older.scss</code> in a <code>@supports</code> block so they only work on devices that <strong>don't</strong> support <code>light-dark()</code> and pass <code>theme-type</code> to <code>mat.theme()</code> so that it doesn't use <code>light-dark()</code> and provides a particular theme always, using css classes to target light and dark themes.  Here's an example of what it looks like:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@supports not (background-color: light-dark(#fff, #000)) {
  :root {
    @include mat.theme((color: (theme-type: light, primary: mat.$azure-palette...
    .dark { 
      @include mat.theme((color: (theme-type: dark, primary: mat.$azure-palette...
      .orange {
        @include mat.theme((color: (theme-type: light, primary: mat.$orange-palette...
      }
      .green {
        @include mat.theme((color: (theme-type: light, primary: mat.$green-palette...
      }
    }
    .orange {
      @include mat.theme((color: (theme-type: light, primary: mat.$orange-palette...
    }
    .green {
      @include mat.theme((color: (theme-type: light, primary: mat.$green-palette...
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>But there's a problem with my scenario.   Since I want to enable switching light/dark</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class=&quot;light&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;dark&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;orange&quot;&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;dark&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;green&quot;&gt;
should be dark green, but may be something else
depending on order of style declarations
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>I can't just target '.green'.   And if I create an element with '.dark.green' and target both '.light .green' and '.dark .green' with different themes, the light green theme will be applied if '.light .green' appears later in the file because they both have the same specificity.  What's nice about using <code>color-scheme</code> is that it is inherited so I dont' have to worry about that.</p>
<p>So I'm messing with <code>@container style()</code> and that is a promising option.   I can do something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>:root {
    --my-color-scheme: light;

    body {
        @container style(--my-color-scheme: light) {
            @include my-theme(mat.$azure-palette, light);
        }
        @container style(--my-color-scheme: dark) {
            @include my-theme(mat.$azure-palette, dark);
        }
    }


    .orange {
        @container style(--my-color-scheme: light) {
            @include my-theme(mat.$orange-palette, light);
        }

        @container style(--my-color-scheme: dark) {
            @include my-theme(mat.$orange-palette, dark);
        }
    }
}
.dark { --my-color-scheme: dark; }
.light { --my-color-scheme: light; }
</code></pre>
<p>A few problems with that.   It works on the container, so it doesn't work if both styles are applied at the same level.   But I can add selectors for that case also and that works.   I can also do the reverse and set values like <code>--my-color-theme: orange</code> and duplicate the styles in my <code>.dark</code> and <code>.light</code> selectors based on those values.</p>
<p>The biggest problem though is that while iOS 16 supports <code>@container</code>, it doesn't support <code>style()</code> for checking the variable values, so I'm out of luck there.  It does work fine on my desktop, but the modern way is much easier anyway in that case.</p>
<p>So I think I will use multiple selectors to try and get the most specific one to work, I should mostly just have the base page style and maybe override for displaying themes, so I don't really need to worry about deeply nesting schemes and themes.  Here if I have a page that is set to <code>.dark.orange</code>, I can set an element to <code>.light.green</code> and the <code>.dark .light.green</code> rule  will take precedence over the <code>.dark .green</code> selector that would also match the element.</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.green,
.green .light,
.light .green,
.light.green,
.dark .light .green,
.dark .green .light,
.dark .light.green {
    @include my-theme(mat.$green-palette, light);
}
</code></pre>

Q: how to get the values from fieldset using javascript? A: <p>Note: Use id to get the component value rather than depending on fieldset.</p>
<p>Here html code goes...</p>
<pre><code>&lt;form name=&quot;myForm&quot; action=&quot;demo_form.asp&quot; onsubmit=&quot;return validateForm()&quot; method=&quot;post&quot;&gt;
First name: &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;fname&quot;&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; value=&quot;Submit&quot;&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Here validation goes... return false will avoid sending request to backend.
If you won't use form name, then use <code>document.getElementById(&lt;id name&gt;).value</code> to get the value</p>
<pre><code>function validateForm()
{
var x=document.forms[&quot;myForm&quot;][&quot;fname&quot;].value;
if (x==null || x==&quot;&quot;)
  {
  alert(&quot;First name must be filled out&quot;);
  return false;
  }
}
</code></pre>
